Prospective replication on new markets. After two external reviews of this section, we
planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis
code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six
new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test’s market
generator. The replication runs R3, C∗ , B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(12) at p = 0, 0.03,
and 0.05, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule’s selected no-churn reference
candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell,
and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling
check was flagged. 20 of the 72 cells were flagged and continued (6 at p = 0, 6 at 0.03, and 8 at
0.05; 8 of them R3’s), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds;
passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell’s frozen winner
unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a
sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired
change from the same market’s no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least
five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. We call the replication prospective-exploratory:
its plan was fixed and stamped before its markets existed, but it is not part of either registered test
and changes no registered decision.

Replication results. Table 10 gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts. With the
primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds for C∗ minus R3 at
p = 0.05 (“met”), for B3 minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), for both at 0.03 (“met”), and for the runner-
up minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), and R3’s audited share of the attacker’s jobs is lower at 0.05 than
without churn on at least five of six markets (“met”). The mean paired changes are −0.146 (d),
−0.142 (q), and −0.118 (u) at p = 0.05, and −0.063, −0.065, and −0.051 at 0.03, all at or below


