markets. The two winner sets differ most in the no-churn baselines, where continued search changed
the pooled C∗ minus R3 difference from 0.044 with the frozen winners to 0.022; the paired changes
therefore carry the search uncertainty of the baseline as well as that of the churn cells. As planned,
the failed expectation is reported without a causal reading.

Ordering and audits on the new markets. At p = 0.05 with the primary winners, C∗ minus
R3 is −0.124 and negative on 6 of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is −0.112 (negative on
6), and B3 minus R3 is −0.030, negative on 6 markets (5 with the all-frozen winners). The sign
reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the
primary winners all three market t intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table 10). R3’s
audited share of the attacker’s jobs falls from between 0.655 and 0.952 without churn to between
0.012 and 0.201 at p = 0.05, and is lower on 6 of six markets. Under its selected attack at 0.05,
R3’s mean gross efficiency is 0.970 (lowest market 0.929) and C∗ ’s is 0.993.

Scope. The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication
adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is
not specific to C∗ . Every claim is restricted to the generator’s market neighbourhood, the four
rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the
seed-bootstrap nor the market t intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the
tested rates, R3’s measured advantage over C∗ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates of
the earlier follow-up, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through p = 0.03 but not
to 0.05, in the earlier follow-up and, in point estimates, on the new markets.




